449 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (After lunch, the Himmeli workshop began. Although himmel are a Finnish decoration, I had never made any kind of himmel myself. So I started practicing with cardboard whistles, from which I moved on to a thin reed. Working required patience, precision and concentration, but was still very addictive and relaxing in its own way. Together with another participant, we also made a joint bigger himmel out of water reeds. It took two people to make a big himmel, because it was much easier when the other one supported or held the himmel so that it didn't move. The material was fine fragile, so could not rush. It was also nice to take a break from time to time, because you often had to hold your breath while threading the thread through the thin reeds. The wonderful thing about the Himmels was that you can easily connect individual Himmels to each other. Many of the participants either connected their own individual channels to each other or had another person make part of a larger Himmel triangle, and when they were combined, a whole triangle was formed. Thus, the creations of this workshop were in their own ways joint works, and each one showed the different handprints of its creators. Helping others and giving advice is also an important part of team building. In the same way as in the Mandala workshop, it was wonderful to help and get help if you didn't know something. I myself stayed to help with the end of another pair of himmel, and we thought together how it should be worked on.)
/Pg 31 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
