487 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The workshop introduced a form of handicraft originally from Mexico, where different colored threads are wrapped around crossed thin sticks/sticks. The end result looks like a flag, and it can be attached to hang or combined with others of the same kind, creating a whole. Traditionally, the yarns are dyed using traditional Mexican methods from dyes made with natural raw materials.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
488 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (We worked in groups of three, and the idea was to switch with your group members after each thread/color round. In this way, each cross/flag would become a work created together by the group. I had not participated in previous workshops and had not met other participants before, so I was a little nervous. But after sitting down with the other participants, speaking came naturally. All the workshop participants were really nice, and the workshop had a very nice and safe atmosphere. I thought it was very interesting that the host Lola had also gathered a handiwork background and shared, for example, the meanings of different colors in Mexican culture. With the help of color theories, we composed what kind of thread combinations we wanted for each cross. Lola also mentioned how "woven mandala" is also a cross-cultural craft, because it is used a lot in other cultures as well. I think this summed up the creations of the workshop very well, because all the participants came from different backgrounds, cultures and countries, but in this workshop we worked together and created something common. My group member was very excited because he has never done this kind of craft. We talked about how the so-called soft crafts have been stereotypically and binary considered suitable only for women, but how should everyone practice the craft they want. Such a workshop was therefore an opportunity for him to try handicrafts, and thus possibly find a new hobby.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
489 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (P8)
/E (P8)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
490 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This, too, was a lot of fun, and really interesting to learn about the context and history behind the craft. The instructions on how to do the specific technique, I think, were clearer in this workshop than in the embroidery one, which definitely helped more people get the hang of it. It was lovely to work again with the handmade yarn, and I found it particularly interesting to learn about how different colours were often used to symbolise different things, like yellow meaning wisdom, green love, and my particular favourite, orange meaning happiness and creativity. It was also nice that we got to take them home. Though I understand that we were meant to be switching the mandalas between us as a team-building exercise, most of us just wanted to work on our own as this seemed a lot more fun to be able to execute the unique ideas we had for colour schemes etc.)
/Pg 35 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
